00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.110 align:start position:0%
healthcare is all about meeting the  needs of a community and data science  can help meet those needs a little  better  specifically when we talk about meeting  the community's needs  what do we mean well there are three  major categories perhaps  the first one is identifying the needs  second it is about optimizing delivery  getting those needs met quickly and  efficiently  and third assessing effectiveness to see  how well you're doing  and see if there's ways that you can  change the services to better meet those  communities needs  let's talk about optimizing delivery and  assessing effectiveness  when it comes to optimizing delivery  there's several aspects that data  science can contribute to  the first one is in terms of just  logistics  for example coordinating the emergency  department functions  so when a person arrives in an emergency  room there's a wide range of tasks to  perform including things like  drawing blood getting x-rays or  ultrasounds obtaining medication  and so on simply determining the order  in which these tasks  should be performed not meaning always  in the same order  but according to what's happening to  this particular person what else is  going on in the er  the time of day determining the order  and matching the staffing the resources  can also make a big difference for  example  some hospitals created a model to  predict patient demand for each category  of lab  tests by time of day and day of week  so it's an optimization model and the  result is that they were  able to reduce average patient wait time  for lab tests  by 75 from one hour to 15 minutes  second there's operating room schedules  the ors in the hospital  are one of the busiest and one of the  most important places in the hospital  they account for sixty percent of  admissions and also sixty percent of the  revenue of the hospital  but in most hospitals the operating  rooms are scheduled by phone calls or  faxes or emails  that makes scheduling slow and it's also  awkward to make changes  for instance when a surgeon ends up not  needing the room for as long as he  anticipated  they are actually also less likely to  make those changes because the process  is awkward  so this is an area that is ripe for tech  for example uc health in colorado went  through a relatively simple process of  creating a smartphone app  that could schedule the operating rooms  and they found that surgeons increased  the number of scheduled blocks that they  released  by nearly fifty percent and did so ten  percent sooner  and that freed up some of the most  valuable resources in the hospital  one of the interesting element of  optimizing delivery is discharge  planning  when a person has received the help they  needed at a hospital and it's time for  them to leave  simple problems like verifying their  insurance or even having a ride home  can slow things down hospitals like  medstar georgetown university hospital  in washington dc  they developed models that help them  predict bottlenecks and discharge as  soon as the patient checks in  this allowed to increase their daily  discharge volume by over 20 percent  and reduce the length of stay that is  the time spent just waiting to get out  by half a day again an immense  improvement in efficiency  that allows the hospital to devote their  resource to other people who need  it as opposed to people who are just  waiting to get out of the building  there is also the matter of assessing  effectiveness  how well is the hospital performing its  functions and can it do so in a way  that's both  more time effective and more cost  effective  well there's several elements to this  one of them  for instance is reducing readmission  hospital visits are expensive and while  it saves money to not keep people there  any longer than they need it really is  problematic and much more expensive  to send somebody out too soon or with  inadequate support  so they end up having to come back again  for the same issue  there's also reducing health problems  for premature babies  for instance at some hospitals  researchers using high frequency  physiological data  such as heart rate and respiration rate  to create algorithms that can predict  when a premature baby is at risk of  infection  and other health complications now  what's really amazing about this  is these algorithms are able to identify  the telltale signs of infection  in changes in their heart rate up 24  hours before the infection takes hold  this allows the hospital time to take  preventive action and potentially save  the life of the newborn  there's also the matter of reducing  travel time for healthcare  depending on where you live you may have  to travel for hours to see  for example a specialist in a rare form  of cancer  or even for something as common as  reproductive health  data science algorithms can help  hospitals in clinics  identity which locations are at greatest  need for specific services  and also determine the schedules that  would best match the availability of the  people who need those services  you can also improve effectiveness by  looking at something like  how well are your program reducing the  waiting time to see health care  professionals  doctors offices are notorious for long  wait times  it would be nice if the patient can come  in and out of there in 15 minutes  the system can use the information that  you had given them previously  that they were able to gather  information passively that the sharing  between  organizations was seamless so that you  didn't have to provide the same  information over and over and over again  and that scheduling could be better  coordinated so that there was less time  waiting in lagging between  all of these things taken together the  ability to identify the places of  greatest need  the ability to coordinate care  effectively the ability to assess  effectiveness  all of these are ways that data science  can help make the provision of health  care more effective and really  more beneficial to the communities that  they serve
 

